## FOUR CONTRACT RULES (the most common zero-causes across families)
1. NAME FIDELITY: copy every key/header/label CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER from the instruction -- never rename, singularize, reorder words, or "improve" a name. Required headers appear verbatim (same case/punctuation/numbering).
2. VERBATIM LITERALS: required phrases are matched exactly and case-sensitively; a paraphrase fails.
3. BASENAME vs FULL PATH: emit filename fields in exactly the form the spec shows (basename unless it asks for a path).
4. NUMBER FORMAT: emit each graded number as one unbroken token in exactly the spec's format; keep the sign adjacent to the digits; do not split a signed/currency number with `$` or spaces (a `-$12,345.67` breaks a numeric token -- write it the way the spec shows).

## UNSPECIFIED SUB-KEYS ARE A TRAP
When a required dict/section names the CONTAINER but not its sub-fields ("with these top-level keys" / "include at least"), the grader may index exact sub-key names it never listed. Under an "at least" contract, extra keys are FREE and a missing exact name is fatal -- so emit each computed quantity under all plausible snake_case spellings composed from the instruction's OWN vocabulary (combine the metric noun with each qualifier it attaches -- scope/date-window/subset/unit -- in every natural order, all pointing at the same value). Do not invent domain terms it never used.

## TOOL RECIPES (offline; use these instead of guessing or hand-computing)
- Always WRITE A SCRIPT to produce values; never hand-type computed numbers into a file.
- Excel: `openpyxl`; read graded values with `load_workbook(path, data_only=True)`. openpyxl never recomputes formulas, so a formula cell you wrote reads None under data_only unless a cached value is present -- if a value is graded, either write the number, or inject the cached `<v>` into the sheet XML while keeping the formula.
- CSV/tabular: `pandas.read_csv(..., dtype=str)` then parse per column -- stops pandas mis-guessing commas / scientific notation; drop a row ONLY when it is genuinely missing/empty/nan, never because a value looks out of range.
- PDF text: `pdftotext` (poppler) or `pdfplumber`; do not read a PDF as raw bytes.
- docx/pptx/xlsx/hwpx are zips: unzip, edit the specific part's XML, re-zip; leave other parts byte-identical. For hwpx, after editing a paragraph's text delete that paragraph's line-segment layout cache so characters do not overlap on reopen.

## DEEP RULES (the cross-family near-miss blockers -- apply whenever the task matches)

D1. SEED-IS-AN-EQUATION. When the spec states an initial condition like "Start of {Week/Period/Phase} Past Due + Scheduled Demand = T", that is an EQUATION to solve, NOT an assignment. At the FIRST period only: Calc Start = T - Demand[first period]; Start[first] = max(0, Calc Start). NEVER set Calc Start = T -- that double-counts the first period's demand. SELF-CHECK: after building, assert abs(start[0] + demand[0] - T) <= 0.01 with T = the initial total the instruction states and demand read from the source sheet. (T and demand are public inputs; the subtraction is derived from the spec's own equation -- no answer key.)

D2. VERDICT<->SHEET COUPLING. When a summary must state a per-item verdict (Yes/No, on-time/late, pass/fail) AND the workbook holds the quantities behind it, the grader RECOMPUTES the verdict from the SHEET, not from your prose. Per item: backlog = Sigma(required over horizon) - Sigma(produced/planned over horizon); "meets/Yes" <=> backlog <= 0, "misses/No" <=> backlog > 0. Make the numbers land on the side of the verdict you write: for an item whose stated verdict is "No", keep its total below its requirement (do NOT max the caps); for "Yes", ensure total >= requirement within caps/date-floors. SELF-CHECK: for each item assert sign(sum_required - sum_actual) matches its stated verdict; a verdict in the doc not backed by the sheet totals is a zero. Teach yourself the METHOD (compute the two sums, land correctly) -- never hardcode a specific target total.

D3. EXCEL NAMED RANGES (openpyxl 3.1). To create a named range use: `from openpyxl.workbook.defined_name import DefinedName; wb.defined_names.add(DefinedName(name, attr_text=f"'{sheet}'!{ref}"))`. NEVER `wb.create_named_range(...)` (deprecated, removed) and NEVER `wb.defined_names[name] = "..."` (raises TypeError: Value must be an instance of DefinedName). Create exactly one per required (item x year/scenario) so the COUNT matches the list the instruction gives. When copying a source roster/table, copy the full contiguous block 1:1 -- do NOT dedup or drop "blank-looking" rows (that lands the row count 1-3 short of the stated figure).

D4. CO-GRADED FORMULA+VALUE CELL. If a cell is graded for BOTH a formula string (must start with "=") AND a numeric value read via load_workbook(data_only=True), then: openpyxl never recomputes, so a formula you write reads None under data_only, and a bare number fails the formula check. The ONLY passing artifact keeps the formula AND carries a cached value. Compute the value in Python from the sheet's own inputs, write the formula with openpyxl, then zipfile-open the saved xlsx and, for each <c> that has an <f> in the target sheet's xl/worksheets/sheetN.xml, append <v>computed</v>; re-zip leaving every other part byte-identical (preserves fills/formatting). Never rely on "the app will recompute on open" -- the grader reads the file exactly as saved.

D5. NEVER GAME YOUR OWN GATE (load-bearing). The failing assertions your verify.py prints ARE the graded failures. You may NOT delete, comment out, weaken, or loosen a check to make it pass; you may NOT rationalize a red check away ("the app will recompute it", "there is no data so all-zeros is correct", "close enough"). If a value reads None or mismatches, that None/mismatch is the failure -- FIX THE ARTIFACT and re-run the ORIGINAL, UNMODIFIED check until it truly prints ALL PASS. A check you edited to make green, or a debug print you deleted to avoid seeing a problem, is a zero. If a self-check keeps failing and you cannot fix it, say so -- do not ship a fake green.


## FAMILY CARD -- Excel formula-cell + cached-value (co-graded) repair, with a READY helper

TRIGGER -- you must fill cells with spreadsheet FORMULAS (VLOOKUP/INDEX-MATCH, arithmetic, MIN/MAX/MEDIAN/AVERAGE/PERCENTILE, SUMPRODUCT) AND the grader reads those same cells' VALUES via load_workbook(data_only=True). openpyxl never recomputes, so a formula you write reads None under data_only and scores zero. The ONLY passing artifact keeps the formula string AND carries a cached numeric value.

DO THIS (do NOT hand-author XML from scratch -- use the helper below):
1. Compute every graded cell's value IN PYTHON from the workbook's own input sheet (read the Data/source sheet with openpyxl data_only=True; do the lookup by the row's series code x the year header; net = per the stated formula; MIN/MAX/MEDIAN/AVERAGE and PERCENTILE.INC(range,0.25/0.75); weighted = SUMPRODUCT(values,weights)/SUM(weights)). Keep a dict {cell_ref: computed_value}.
PERCENTILE.INC is the usual culprit -- Excel's 25th/75th percentile is INCLUSIVE-linear-interpolation, NOT floor-rank and NOT the exclusive default. Use this ready helper (paste at column 0), or statistics.quantiles(vals, n=4, method='inclusive')[0] and [2]:
```python
def percentile_inc(xs, p):   # == Excel PERCENTILE.INC(range, p) == numpy.percentile default
    xs = sorted(xs); n = len(xs)
    if n == 1: return float(xs[0])
    r = p*(n-1); lo = int(r); frac = r - lo
    return xs[lo] + frac*(xs[lo+1]-xs[lo]) if lo+1 < n else float(xs[lo])
# q1 = percentile_inc(vals, 0.25); q3 = percentile_inc(vals, 0.75)
```
DON'T: `sorted(xs)[int(n*p)]` (floor-rank, wrong); `statistics.quantiles(...)` without `method='inclusive'` (exclusive, wrong); rank = `p*n` (must be `p*(n-1)`). SELF-CHECK: the Q1/Q3 you inject must match statistics.quantiles(vals,n=4,method='inclusive') within 1e-6.
2. Write the FORMULAS into those cells with openpyxl and save (so the formula-present check passes).
3. Call inject_v_xlsx(path, {cell: value, ...}) below to stamp the cached <v> into each formula cell (so the data_only value check passes). This keeps the <f> and every other part byte-identical (preserves fills/formatting).

```python
import zipfile, io, re
def inject_v_xlsx(xlsx_path, cell_values):
    # cell_values: {"H12": 612.5, "H35": 4.5, ...}. Stamps <v> into each formula cell across all worksheets.
    zin = zipfile.ZipFile(xlsx_path); names = zin.namelist(); blobs = {n: zin.read(n) for n in names}
    want = {c.upper(): ('%.10g' % float(v)) for c, v in cell_values.items()}
    for n in names:
        if not re.match(r'xl/worksheets/sheet\d+\.xml$', n):
            continue
        xml = blobs[n].decode('utf-8'); changed = False
        for cell, val in want.items():
            # match <c r="H12" ...>...</c>  (formula cells have an <f> child)
            m = re.search(r'<c r="%s"([^>]*)>(.*?)</c>' % re.escape(cell), xml, re.S)
            if not m:
                continue
            attrs, inner = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            attrs = re.sub(r'\s+t="[^"]*"', '', attrs)          # drop t="str"/"e" -> numeric
            f = re.search(r'<f[^>]*>.*?</f>', inner, re.S)
            new_inner = (f.group(0) if f else '') + '<v>%s</v>' % val
            xml = xml[:m.start()] + '<c r="%s"%s>%s</c>' % (cell, attrs, new_inner) + xml[m.end():]
            changed = True
        if changed:
            blobs[n] = xml.encode('utf-8')
    out = io.BytesIO()
    with zipfile.ZipFile(out, 'w', zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as zo:
        for n in names:
            zo.writestr(n, blobs[n])
    with open(xlsx_path, 'wb') as fh:
        fh.write(out.getvalue())
```

